course: everyday
lesson: 195
title: Why people lie, and what is not a lie
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A lie is saying something you know is false in order to make someone believe it. The wish to fool someone is what makes it a lie.
explain: People lie for understandable reasons: to escape trouble, to be admired, to get something they want, or to cover for a friend. Understanding the reason does not make the lie right, but it helps you be fair and helps you choose the truth yourself.
explain: Lots of untrue words are not lies at all: a made-up story everyone knows is made up, an actor playing a villain, a little child's pretend dragon, or «I've told you a million times!» Nobody is being fooled.
explain: Example: a boy in Ohio breaks a window and says «It wasn't me» because he is scared. Now he must invent more lies to cover the first one, and when the truth comes out he is in trouble for the window and for the lie. Owning up early would have kept the trouble small.
fact: A lie means trying to make someone believe something you know is false.
fact: Fear of being punished is the most common reason people lie.
fact: Owning up early usually makes the trouble smaller than being caught later.
fact: One lie often has to be covered by more lies.
fact: Some people make up stories about themselves to be liked or admired, but real friends like you for who you really are.
fact: Lying to get something you want is still lying, even if the thing seems small.
fact: Saying «Mum said I could» when she did not is a trick to make a lie hard to argue with, so check with the adult yourself.
fact: Loyalty to a friend does not mean lying for them; covering up can let harm go on and drags you into the trouble.
fact: Saying «I don't understand» is honest and brave, and pretending to understand means you miss the help you need.
fact: Very young children still mix up wishing, pretending and what really happened, so their tall tales are imagination, not wicked lies.
fact: Stories, plays, films and games of pretend are shared make-believe, not lies, because everyone knows they are not real.
fact: Exaggerating for effect, like «a million times», is not a lie because listeners know it is not meant literally.
fact: Lying can become a habit, and honesty can become a habit too with practice and help.
fact: You can say no politely, like «Thanks, but I can't come», without inventing an excuse.
exercise: A friend says he did not break the window, only because he is scared of being punished. What is the most common reason people lie? | Fear of getting into trouble | Because they enjoy fooling people | Because they forgot what really happened | Because lying is easier to remember
exercise: Why does owning up early usually help? | It keeps the trouble smaller than being caught later | It means there will be no consequences at all for what happened | It makes the broken thing fix itself | It lets you blame someone else later
exercise: A classmate says she has a pony and a pool at home to sound impressive. Why might she do it? | She wants to be liked or admired | She has forgotten what her home is like | She is trying to sell you a pony | She thinks ponies are not impressive
exercise: A boy says he did his homework so he can play video games. Is that a lie? | Yes, lying to get something you want is still lying | No, because a game is only a small thing, so it doesn't really count | No, because he plans to do it later anyway | Only if his parents find out about it
exercise: Your sister says «Mum said I could have the cake» but you are not sure. What is wise? | Check with Mum yourself before going along with it | Believe her, because she used Mum's name | Eat some cake too, so that you share the trouble if there is any | Tell her she is lying in front of everyone
exercise: A friend asks you to lie to the teacher to cover for him. What is true about loyalty? | You can stay kind to a friend without lying for him | A loyal friend always lies when asked | Covering up never drags you into trouble | Telling the truth about him means you were never really friends at all
exercise: Why is it better to say «I don't understand» in class than to pretend? | Pretending means you miss the help you need | Teachers get cross when you ask anything they have already explained | Pretending makes the lesson easier later | Saying it shows you did not listen at all
exercise: A four-year-old says a dragon ate his biscuits. What is going on? | He is still learning the difference between pretend and real | He is a wicked liar who must be punished so he learns his lesson | He really saw a dragon in the kitchen | He is trying to trick you for money
exercise: At story time you tell a made-up tale about a talking dog. Is that lying? | No, everyone knows the story is made up | Yes, because dogs cannot really talk | Yes, all untrue sentences are lies | Only if you tell it to younger children
exercise: An actor plays a villain in a film. Is the actor lying? | No, the audience knows it is a performance | Yes, because he is not really a villain | Yes, because he says untrue things on screen | Only if he plays a villain very well
exercise: Someone lies so often that he does it even when there is no need. What is true? | Lying can become a habit, and habits can be changed | He was born a liar and can never change | Lying more often makes the habit wear out and stop by itself | A habit of lying only hurts other people
exercise: Your friend says «I've told you a million times!» What is she doing? | Exaggerating to show strong feeling | Telling you an exact number | Lying to trick you about how many times it was | Counting how often she has spoken
exercise: You do not want to go to a party. Which answer is honest and polite? | «Thanks for inviting me, but I can't come» | «I'm sick», when you are really fine | «My mum won't let me», when she would | «I'll be away», when you will be at home
exercise: What makes something a lie rather than a mistake? | The speaker knows it is false and wants you to believe it | The sentence turns out to be untrue | The sentence turns out to be untrue, even if the speaker believed it | The listener did not like hearing it
test: A girl in Texas tells her class her uncle is a famous astronaut, which is not true, because she wants friends. What would help her most? | Learning that people can like her as she really is | Making her story even more exciting | Never talking to anyone at school again | Asking the whole class to promise to believe everything she says
test: Tom hid a broken vase and now has to invent a cat, a storm and an open window to explain it. What does this show? | One lie often needs more lies to cover it | Lies are easy to keep going for a long time | The cat is the one who should own up | Small lies never grow into bigger ones
test: Which of these is NOT a lie? | A comedian telling a silly made-up story on stage | Saying you fed the dog when you did not | Telling a teacher you were ill when you were not | Saying Dad allowed something when he did not
test: A boy in Leeds keeps a secret for a friend who has been taking other children's snacks. Why is lying for him a problem? | Covering up lets the harm go on and drags you in | It is fine because friends must always stick together | It only matters if the snacks were expensive | The other children will never find out anyway
test: Your little cousin, aged three, says she flew to the Moon last night. How should you take it? | As imagination, since small children mix up pretend and real | As a serious lie that must be punished | As proof that she really can fly | As a sign that she wants to trick you and see if you believe her
test: A girl says she understood the fractions lesson because she felt embarrassed. What will most likely happen? | She will miss the help she needs with fractions | She will understand fractions later by magic, without any help at all | The teacher will know anyway and not mind | She will get every fractions question right
test: A boy has told small lies for weeks and now lies without thinking. What can he do? | Practise small truths; honesty gets easier with practice | Nothing, because once a liar always a liar, and it can't be undone | Tell bigger lies so the small ones stop | Wait for the habit to vanish on its own
test: Which reason makes something a lie? | Wanting someone to believe what you know is false | Saying something in a funny voice so that people laugh | Saying a number that is too big | Telling a story at bedtime
test: A boy is invited to a party he does not want to go to. Why does he not need to invent an illness? | He can say no politely without giving a reason | Parties always need a medical excuse | Saying no is ruder than any lie | An invented illness can never be found out, so it is the kindest way
test: broke the lamp, mums gonna kill me lol, should i just hide it | Own up early, it makes the trouble smaller | Hide it, she may never notice it's gone from the shelf | Blame the dog | Glue it and say nothing
test: my 3 yr old bro says a dragon ate his sock, is he lying? | No, little kids mix up pretending and what happened | Yes, he's a wicked liar | Yes, punish him for lying | Yes, socks can't be eaten
test: told u a million times!! is that a lie | No, it's exaggeration and everyone knows it's not literal | Yes, it wasn't really a million | Yes, any number that's wrong is a lie, even if you're just cross | Only if you said it angrily
test: dont wanna go to his party, gotta make up an excuse right | No, you can just say thanks, but I can't come | Yes, say you're ill | Yes, say your nan died | Say you'll come and then don't
explain: A boy in Ohio lied that he was at a friend's house when he went to the lake; he fell in and nobody knew where to look for him, and it took hours to find him cold and scared.
explain: A girl in Leeds lied to avoid trouble about a broken phone; when it came out, her parents trusted her less, and she lost the phone for a month.
fact: When people lie about where they are going, nobody knows where to look if something goes wrong.
fact: Many lies are told to avoid trouble, but they usually lead to more trouble when found out.
test: gonna say i'm at jack's but really going to the lake | Tell the truth, if something goes wrong no one knows where you are | It's fine, nothing will happen, you're only going for an hour or so | Tell your little brother only | Say it then text later
test: why do ppl even lie | Often to avoid trouble or look good, but it usually backfires | Because lying is always good | Only bad people lie | People lie to make others happy every single time, never for themselves
test: lied to avoid trouble, now it's worse | Tell the truth now, the sooner the better | Add another lie to cover it | Blame someone else | Keep quiet forever
test: did u hear Ryan told his mum he was at Ben's but went to the lake, fell in, n they had helicopters out all night!! | Tell adults the truth about where you go; the helicopters may be exaggerated | Lying is fine if you're back by tea | Lakes are safe if you lie well | Helicopters come for every lie a kid tells
test: apparently Mia says she's met a famous footballer like 5 times, she's such a liar, tell everyone | Don't spread it; people make up stories to be liked, and gossip hurts | Tell everyone so she stops | It must be true, nobody would invent something like that about a footballer | Make up a bigger story to beat hers
test: my brother's mate says his little sister told everyone a dragon lives in their shed lol, she's a liar right | No; very young children mix up pretend and real, it's imagination | Yes, she's telling a wicked lie | She should be punished for lying | Dragons might really live in sheds
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a lie? | Saying what one knows is false in order to make someone believe it | Saying anything untrue, whether or not anyone is being fooled | Telling a story or acting in a play that everyone knows | Exaggerating, as in "I've told you a million times"
exam: Identify the most common reason for which people tell lies. | Fear of being punished | A wish to make others happy | A habit of telling stories | Boredom during the day
exam: It is given that a person has told a lie to escape trouble. Determine the course of action that usually keeps the trouble smallest. | Owning up early | Adding further lies | Blaming another person | Keeping silent forever
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning loyalty to a friend. | It does not mean lying for them, since cover-ups let harm go on | It means always covering for them, whatever they have done | It means lying for them only when an adult is asking | It means repeating whatever story the friend has told
